+| 85 | [shared] webxr: Expose fixed foveation on projection layers | Fixed foveation - rendering the periphery at reduced resolution where the eye cannot see detail - is the single most effective performance lever on standalone headsets, and the WebXR Layers API exposes it as a mutable fixedFoveation attribute (0..1) that the browser compositor applies at zero engine cost. Godot had no API for it on any WebXR path. Adds WebXRInterface.fixed_foveation, plumbed exactly like display_refresh_rate: the glue clamps and stores the request, applies it when a projection layer is created (GL and WebGPU binding paths both) and live mid-session, and the getter reads the layer attribute back because the runtime may clamp the request. The support check is per-instance ("fixedFoveation" in layer), not prototype-based: Quest Browser 149 exposes the attribute on the created XRProjectionLayer while its XRCompositionLayer prototype lacks it. Verified on-device (set 1.0 reads back 1.0, periphery softens). Unsupporting browsers see no behavior change. Strong upstream candidate: mirrors an existing WebXRInterface property pattern, no WebGPU dependency | ~65 across 8 modules/webxr files (all SHARED_OK) | not yet |
