+| 93 | [shared] rendering: Extract the cluster box scale from the basis columns | ClusterBuilderRD::add_box folded a decal's or reflection probe's node scale into its half-extents using the length of each basis ROW. The per-axis scale of a basis is the length of its COLUMNS; rows only agree when no rotation is present, and xform is view_xform * p_transform, so a rotation is always present. Anything scaled through its node transform rather than through its size property was therefore clipped to a proxy box built from a mixture of its axes - one that changed shape as the camera turned, so it read as jagged edges while orbiting rather than as a wrong size. Minimal repro (floor, one light, one opaque decal, one camera): the same 11x4x11 box authored via size spans the authored rows 83..438, authored via a basis scale it spanned 128..352; after the fix both span 83..438. Not WebGPU-specific - it reproduces identically on native Vulkan. Upstream-PR candidate | 18 | not yet |
+| 94 | [shared] webgpu: Implement timestamp queries for GPU profiling | viewport_get_measured_render_time_gpu read 0 on the web, so the backend could not be profiled at all and every perf number had to be taken from native Vulkan as a stand-in - a different driver, which by construction cannot show WebGPU-specific cost. WebGPU has no encoder-level writeTimestamp: timestamps may only be written at pass boundaries. The engine always captures BETWEEN passes (capture_timestamp refuses to run inside an open list), so each capture rides its own empty compute pass, which costs nothing and lands exactly where it was asked for. Two traps: the pinned emdawnwebgpu bindings marshal BOTH pass write indices unconditionally, so WGPU_QUERY_SET_INDEX_UNDEFINED reaches JS as a literal 0xFFFFFFFF and invalidates the pass - the port cannot express 'no end-of-pass timestamp', so the unwanted write points at a scratch slot; and resolving per command-buffer-end captured queries that had not been written yet (the engine submits several per frame), so the resolve is one dedicated encoder submitted after the frame. Readback is async (no synchronous map on the web), so results are served from the last completed map - profiling numbers a frame or two old. Without the feature the pool stays a benign token and reads zeros, exactly as before. Measured working on STOCK Chrome, no developer flags: steady 0.87-0.92 ms on the stress scene. Chrome quantizes timestamps to ~100us, which is far below a frame's cost but does hide sub-0.1ms effects | 7 | not yet |
