+| 130 | [shared] webgpu: Bake a native-subgroup translation next to the lowered one | The clustered light loop uses subgroup ops for wave coherence; the pinned tint cannot read them, so patch 64 lowers them to exact wave-of-1 form. A newer tint (Dawn v20260831) reads them but (a) enforces WGSL's subgroup_uniformity rule, which Godot's cluster-dependent control flow violates by design, and (b) crashes nondeterministically (lower/texture.cc:581) on the area-light variants that translate fine today - so adopting it outright would regress fidelity. Instead the bake keeps two translators: the baseline blob is unchanged, and stages whose SPIR-V declares a GroupNonUniform capability additionally run through GODOT_TINT_PATH_SUBGROUPS with --allow-non-uniform-subgroup-operations (retrying the flaky assert a few times) after a C++ port of the sampled-image retype that reader needs; the result is stored as a per-stage second blob via the container's shader extra-data hooks (format version 2). The driver picks the native blob only when the device has the subgroups feature AND the page opts in with ?subgroups; layouts are identical between blobs. Measured: 150 of the Forward+ stress export's clustered fragment stages carry a native blob (+2.3 MB pck), the stress scene creates 15 of them at boot (WGPUPERF sg_new) and they pass WebGPU validation - but on Chrome 152's D3D12 backend the GPU process then dies in dxcompiler.dll (exit code 0x80000003, a DXC assert on the generated wave-op HLSL; minidump kept), which loses the device for the whole page. The same modules with the wave ops lowered run fine, and tint's own SPIR-V writer translates all of them, so this is a D3D12/DXC-specific bug - reproduced offline with Chrome's exact dxcompiler.dll (126 of 148 modules abort in the hlsl-validate-wave-sensitivity pass at -O1+; -O0 and DXC 1.8 compile them all = a DXC 1.9 regression with no lexical workaround). Native selection therefore ships opt-in until Chrome carries a fixed DXC (repro