+| 185 | misc: Check a warm frame's WebGPU hygiene in the local gates | The driver patches that keep a warm frame cheap had no check that could catch them sliding back. A new gate captures one warm frame of the mobile export with WebGPU Inspector injected before any page script and grades it with the inspector's own frame-issue rules: it fails on mid-frame pipeline, shader, buffer, texture or bind-group creation, fragmented writeBuffer calls and many submits. On the gated mobile export: 275 commands, 21 writeBuffer, 3 submits, clean; `?nowritebatch` fails it with fragmented-buffer-writes x46 (66 writeBuffer). The Forward+ export reads 683, 4 and 3, clean, and 795, 116 and 3 with `?nowritebatch`, which fails with x112. Skipped when no inspector copy is vendored, like the IWER glue check. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 186 | misc: Read render-bundle commands from the capture's descriptors | The hygiene gate looked for a render bundle's commands on the captured object, but a saved capture keeps them in the bundle's descriptor, so every draw inside a bundle was invisible to the rules that follow pass state. The gate's verdict was unaffected (none of its failing rules read bundles); the printed rules were wrong: on the Forward+ export overwritten-before-read read 59 instead of 7 and unread-store 16 instead of 9, the extra results being the buffers bundled draws read. Also corrects row 185: its Forward+ x112 is the `?nowritebatch` reading. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 187 | webgpu: Skip pipeline and buffer binds that repeat what is bound | WebGPU keeps the pipeline and the vertex and index buffers bound across draws and pipeline changes, but the driver encoded every bind the engine issued. A warm frame of the mobile export carried 5 compute `setPipeline` calls for the pipeline already bound (the engine rebinds between dispatches of one effect) and 18 `setVertexBuffer` calls for vertex slot 3 holding the same buffer, inside the cached bundle and on the direct path alike. The command buffer now remembers the open pass's pipeline, vertex buffers and index buffer, cleared whenever a pass begins, and skips a bind that matches; a repeated pipeline also no longer marks every bind group dirty, since the groups bound for it still apply. Bundle records start at the pass's start, so they see the same state. Measured with the hygiene capture: redundant pipeline and buffer binds 23 to 0, with and without bundles; `?nostateskip` restores them (control). PlayCanvas's WebGPU device does the same tracking. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 188 | webgpu: Stop capturing pass positions whose streams do not repeat | Every render pass was buffered for bundling, compared against the framebuffer's cached streams, hashed byte by byte and then replayed, whether or not its stream ever came back. Under a moving camera with unique materials it never does: the cross-engine benchmark at 4,000 unique objects spent about 2 ms of CPU a frame on capture that `?nobundles` does not pay. Each framebuffer now counts cache hits per pass position within a frame (its first pass, its second, ...); a position that hits on fewer than a quarter of 32 captures encodes directly for 240 frames, then is measured again, so a static shadow cascade keeps its bundle beside a churning one. Measured, unique mode 4,000 objects: CPU 13.6 to 11.7 ms a frame (`?nobundles` 11.3); batch mode 8,000 objects keeps its ~260 cache hits per 120 frames and its 4.4 ms. `?alwayscapture` restores capturing everything. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 189 | webgpu: Cache each uniform set's last layout lookup | Every draw's bind-group flush ran hash lookups: the set's group for the shader's layout, whether the layout carries the push-constant ring entry, and one per dynamic offset. The profile showed them as a ClassDB HashMap function (the linker folds identical HashMap code), 0.54 ms a frame at 4,000 unique objects. Each shader now keeps a bit per set carrying the ring entry, and each uniform set keeps the layout it was last flushed for, its group and which of its dynamic offsets that layout declares; the hash maps stay as the fallback when the layout changes, and a ring replacement clears the entry with the cached groups. Measured, unique mode 4,000 objects: that lookup 17.3 to 0.1 ms per second, CPU 11.7 to 11.25 ms a frame. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 190 | webgpu: Encode each render pass's commands in one JavaScript call | Each render-pass command crossed from wasm into the emdawnwebgpu bindings on its own: a transition, a heap-view refresh, an object lookup per handle and a BigInt conversion of every 64-bit offset - about 1.7 ms a frame at 4,000 unique objects, measured by name in a profiling build. Pipeline, bind group, vertex and index buffer, draw, indirect draw, viewport, scissor and blend-constant commands now append u32 ops to a per-command-buffer stream, and the pass hands the stream to one JavaScript function that walks it and calls the encoder, before executing a bundle and before ending the pass. Dynamic offsets are copied into a non-shared typed array for `setBindGroup`. Bundle recording still calls the bindings directly. Measured, unique mode 4,000 objects: CPU 11.4 to 10.1 ms a frame; `?nojsops` encodes command by command again. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 191 | webgpu: Compare dynamic slices a word at a time | The range-diffed slice flush found the changed span by calling memcmp on 64-byte steps, and memcmp is a byte loop in this build, so an unchanged slice still cost a byte compare per byte. On the 500-object benchmark that was a 720 KB slice every frame and a 2 MB slice every other frame: 0.8 ms of a 1.7 ms frame. The first and last differing words are now found with 64-bit loads and the span widened to 64-byte boundaries as before, so the uploaded bytes are a superset of the changed ones, exactly as before. Checked on 200,000 random byte pairs under wasm (span covers every change, within one word). Scan self time 25.5 to 13.9 ms per second. | 0 | n/a (fork-only) |
+| 192 | [shared] renderer: Name the written range when flushing a mapped buffer | The WebGPU driver diffs a persistently mapped buffer's slice against the last upload to find what changed, because `buffer_flush` carries no range; on small scenes that scan of capacity the engine never wrote was the largest CPU item (a 720 KB and a 2 MB slice, about 0.6 ms of a 1.7 ms frame at 500 objects). `RenderingDeviceDriver::buffer_flush_range` (default: the whole `buffer_flush`, so Vulkan, D3D12 and Metal are untouched) and `RenderingDevice::buffer_flush_range` (inline in the header: `rendering_device.cpp` stays untouched) let the instance-buffer fills of both scene renderers, their shadow lists and the canvas item buffer name the bytes they wrote; the WebGPU driver diffs only inside that window. Measured on the cross-engine benchmark: 500 objects batch 1.71 to 1.06 ms (PlayCanvas 1.12), 500 unique 2.74 to 2.41 ms, 4,000 unique 10.0 to 9.7 ms; boot frames and a 2,000-object frame complete. `?noflushrange` diffs the whole slice again. Allowlist += rendering_device(_driver).h, renderer_canvas_render_rd.cpp. | 25 | not yet (upstream-shaped: an optional driver hint) |
