+| 194 | webgpu: Upload only the push-constant slots that changed | The ring is reused from slot 0 every frame and every used byte was uploaded each submission: 256-byte slots for 16-byte mobile draw constants, about 2 MB a frame on the XR benchmark (2,000 shadowed boxes, both eyes). The shadow now keeps what the GPU copy holds, a push compares its values with the slot's and only changed slots are uploaded, as ordered spans merged across gaps of up to four slots; a reallocated ring zeroes the shadow to match the new buffer. XR benchmark: 243 MB to 0.24 MB of push-constant uploads per 120 frames; frame callback at 4x CPU throttle 23.2 to 15.7 ms (three interleaved pairs, all one direction); both eyes pixel-identical to the old path. `?nopcdiff` restores the full upload. | 0 | - |
+| 195 | webgpu: Skip literal operands when collecting unused shader resources | The dead-binding pass counted every instruction word as an id use, so a constant whose value equalled an orphaned type's id kept that type alive. In the Forward+ prepass variant a constant 1024 kept array type %1024 and its element struct VoxelGIData after the VoxelGI block was removed; Tint emitted the bare struct with `@stride(16) @internal(disable_validation__ignore_stride)` and the browser rejected the module at XR session start. Literal operands (constants, type widths and counts, storage classes, composite indices, merge controls, switch cases, the ext-inst number) no longer count as uses. Both CI censuses unchanged; the Forward+ XR session loses its two WGSL parse errors. | 0 | - |
+| 196 | webgpu: Compare uploads and bundle streams 64 bytes at a time | The dynamic-slice diff and the render-bundle identity check scan whole unchanged ranges every frame (instance data and draw records barely change between frames), with 8-byte loads and a byte-loop memcmp respectively. Whole 64-byte blocks are now compared with four WebAssembly SIMD compares (plain 64-bit loads without SIMD), then 8-byte words locate the difference inside the first differing block; the bundle check uses the same helper. 200,000 random range pairs match the old results; a cache-hot 1.3 MB unchanged compare takes 0.031 ms instead of 0.123 at -Os. On the XR benchmark profile the two compares fall from about 15 to 8 ms per second of wall time. | 0 | - |
