+| 203 | [shared] renderer: Repeat the last directional shadow split in the unused ones | With the orthogonal (1 split) or 2-split directional shadow mode, `LightStorage` wrote 0 offsets for the unused splits and built their matrices from shadow transforms that were never computed. The scene and fog shaders pick a split by comparing depth against each offset and fall through to split 4, so every fragment past the shadow range was shaded with that never-computed matrix. Desktop GPUs returned a finite sample that the distance fade then hid; on a Galaxy S25 Ultra (Adreno 830, Chrome 156) the ground past the range drew pure black with a clean console (found by the Dino Pals game, phone A/B: ?noshadow and the 4-split mode fixed it, the orthogonal mode brought it back). Unused splits now repeat the last real split's offset, matrix and biases, so the fall-through samples a valid split and the fade works as intended; it also stops the 2-split blend from mixing in the unset split at its far edge. Renderer code shared with native (upstream-relevant). |
+| 204 | webgpu: Answer vertex and index buffer readbacks from a CPU copy | WebGPU maps buffers only asynchronously and buffer_get_data() reads synchronously, so every GPU readback returned the download buffer's unfilled contents: Mesh.surface_get_arrays() and PrimitiveMesh.get_mesh_arrays() gave garbage (Dino Pals: shards from runtime primitives). Vertex and index buffers now keep a CPU copy, written as copies and clears are encoded, and a download from one fills the CPU buffer from it; a download from a GPU-written buffer (storage, dynamic) reports once that it is unsupported. Costs memory equal to the mesh data. Device check `mesh_readback` (SphereMesh vertex 0 and index range) passes; `?noreadbackmirror` restores the old behaviour and fails it. | 0 | - |
